{"id":"smith-wesson-model-29","slug":"smith-wesson-model-29","name":"Smith & Wesson Model 29","aliases":["SWM29","S&WM29","SW M29","S&W 29","S&W M29","S&W QSPR","S&W M-29","Model 29","S&W Model 29","S&W Model 629","Dirty Harry gun","Smith & Wesson M29","Smith and Wesson 29","Smith and Wesson M29","Smith and Wesson M-29","Smith & Wesson Model 629","Smith and Wesson Model 29","Quiet Special Purpose Revolver","Quiet Special Purpose Revolvers","Smith & Wesson Model 629 Stealth Hunter"],"kind":"firearm","category":"revolvers","subcategory":"Revolver","description":"The Smith & Wesson Model 29 is a six-shot, double-action revolver chambered for the .44 Magnum cartridge and manufactured by the United States company Smith & Wesson.","history":"Cartridge developer Elmer Keith's achievement in maximizing the power and performance of the .44 Special was the inspiration and driving force behind the introduction of the .44 Magnum by Smith & Wesson. He intended the new round to be used in sidearms of hunters of large, dangerous game, rather than for self-defense; it proved successful in both.\nS&W's production of a large N-frame revolver in .44 Magnum began in 1955; the Model 29 designation was applied in 1957. At the time of its introduction, the Model 29 was the most powerful production handgun.\nIt remained primarily the province of some handgun enthusiasts, law enforcement personnel, and hunters until Clint Eastwood made it famous in the 1971 film Dirty Harry. After its release and several sequels, retailers had difficulty keeping the Model 29 in stock.\nIn the late 1990s, Smith & Wesson discontinued series production of many models of revolvers, including the basic Model 29; since then, the model has been manufactured in as many as 10 limited or \"custom\" configurations.","development":"The Model 29 will chamber and fire .44 Special and .44 Russian cartridges, as the .44 Magnum was developed from the .44 Special and the .44 Special was developed from the .44 Russian. The Magnum case is slightly longer to prevent magnum rounds from being chambered and fired in handguns chambered for the .44 Special.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":".44 Magnum"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":".44 Special"},{"label":"Action","value":"Double-action"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"Six round cylinder"},{"label":"Sights","value":"Open, adjustable rear"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","value":"41.5 oz (4 in bbl)","numeric":41.5,"unit":"oz"},{"label":"Weight","value":"45 oz (6 in bbl)","numeric":45,"unit":"oz"},{"label":"Weight","value":"Both of these weights are for the traditional S&W style without a barrel underlug."},{"label":"Length","value":"9.3 in (Barrel 4 in)","numeric":9.3,"unit":"in"},{"label":"Length","value":"12 in (Barrel 6+1/2 in)","numeric":12,"unit":"in"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"3 in","numeric":3,"unit":"in"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"4 in","numeric":4,"unit":"in"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"5 in","numeric":5,"unit":"in"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"6 in","numeric":6,"unit":"in"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"Smith & Wesson"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1955–present"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"United States"},{"label":"In Service","value":"1955–present"}]}],"specIndex":{"Cartridge":".44 Magnum","Action":"Double-action","Feed System":"Six round cylinder","Sights":"Open, adjustable rear","Weight":"41.5 oz (4 in bbl)","Length":"9.3 in (Barrel 4 in)","Barrel Length":"3 in","Manufacturer":"Smith & Wesson","Produced":"1955–present","Origin":"United States","In Service":"1955–present"},"images":{"hero":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/Smith_%26_Wesson_Model_29_retouched.jpg","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smith_%26_Wesson_Model_29_retouched.jpg","width":1245,"height":772,"license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licenseUrl":"http: